WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.544
C++ programs use different error-handling styles.

2
00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:03.998
Some rely on exceptions.

3
00:00:03.998 --> 00:00:10.176
Some use return values, status objects, expected-like types, error codes, callbacks, or combinations of these patterns.

4
00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:14.174
The security question is not which style sounds best in isolation.

5
00:00:14.174 --> 00:00:19.263
The question is whether failure leaves the program in a predictable and valid state.

6
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:22.534
RAII can simplify cleanup during exceptions and early returns.

7
00:00:22.534 --> 00:00:31.983
If a lock, file, temporary allocation, or handle is owned by an object with a destructor, cleanup does not depend on every error path remembering a

8
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:33.073
manual release call.

9
00:00:33.073 --> 00:00:37.798
That makes secure cleanup easier to review and less vulnerable to future edits.

10
00:00:37.798 --> 00:00:39.615
Partial initialization should be visible.

11
00:00:39.615 --> 00:00:44.340
An object that failed to initialize should not pretend to be fully usable.

12
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:52.336
Constructors, factory functions, status-returning initialization, and invariant checks should make it clear when an object is ready and when it is

13
00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:52.699
not.

14
00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:58.878
A half-ready object can cause later code to make decisions based on missing or stale state.

15
00:00:58.878 --> 00:01:04.330
Error handling should avoid continuing with stale data, partial output, invalid pointers, or untrusted defaults.

16
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:10.145
A failed parse should not leave old values active unless that behavior is deliberate and documented.

17
00:01:10.145 --> 00:01:13.416
A failed write should not be logged as success.

18
00:01:13.416 --> 00:01:17.414
A failed security check should not silently choose a permissive fallback.

19
00:01:17.414 --> 00:01:21.411
Diagnostics should provide useful context without exposing secrets or misleading operators.

20
00:01:21.411 --> 00:01:26.136
A good log can identify the operation, component, status category, and recovery path.

21
00:01:26.136 --> 00:01:32.315
It should not dump credentials, sensitive data, or internal state that does not belong in operational records.

22
00:01:32.315 --> 00:01:34.495
Safe failure is a design property.

23
00:01:34.495 --> 00:01:40.310
Decide what each component guarantees after failure, test those paths, and keep cleanup automatic where practical.

24
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:49.033
Review should trace not only the successful path but also the path where input is bad, allocation fails, a dependency is unavailable, or concurrent

25
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:49.760
shutdown begins.
